{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem","id":467762,"title":"Four Races of Irem","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-03T12:36:46-07:00","id":467762,"title":"Four Races of Irem","content":"Sothis Ascends posits people of Nubian/Medjay descent as one of the &quot;four races of Irem,&quot; helping us visualize the ethnic makeup of the Nameless Empire. Anyone want to help a poor soul understand the diversity of lost Irem? There's the whole controversy over Ancient Egypt's &quot;race,&quot; which I would hope to avoid here.<br /> <br /> We know it was North African, with holdings into Mesopotamia, so it's a safe bet to assume that conquered peoples from the Levant could be one. I'm a little fuzzy on how the modern Egyptian ethnic group came to be (commonly perceived as Arab in descent), so would that be around in ~5000BCE?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T16:34:40-07:00","id":467958,"title":"","content":"Am I completely misremembering, or aren't they spelled out in the Mummy Corebook?<br /> <br /> Anyways. The four races of Irem are, away from the game's fiction, actually the four races recognized by Ancient Egyptians.<br /> <br /> In modern terms, and as they were known in the First Turn<br /> <br /> Egyptians / Rem-en-Kemet<br /> Libyans / Tjemehu<br /> Syrians / Byblos<br /> Nubians / Medjay","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T17:19:59-07:00","id":468011,"title":"","content":"Didn't the corebook also mentioned that there were some people from Asia Minor as well?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-03T19:16:46-07:00","id":468072,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Felipe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=468011#post468011\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Didn't the corebook also mentioned that there were some people from Asia Minor as well?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think under the racial classifications present, they'd fall under Byblos (Syrian). It's hard to know because most of what we know of the Hittites comes post them being shaped by Assyrian colonizers.<br /> <br /> And needless to say the Turkic peoples who are the majority of Asia Minor's inhabitants today were nowhere near the region in the Third Millennium bce.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 04-03-2015, 10:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-04T12:14:50-07:00","id":468617,"title":"","content":"Yeah, we have to separate modern ideas of race versus ancient Egyptian and thus Iremite ideas of race, and again modern from Victorian/Orientalist.<br /> <br /> An Iremite would ask &quot;Are you Tjemehu or Byblos?&quot; An Orientalist would ask &quot;Are you Negro or Semitic?&quot; And a modern scholar would ask &quot;Are you Hebrew, Arab, Hittite, Sumerian, etc&quot;.<br /> <br /> Our categories are different from theirs. It pretty much came down to &quot;Us, the southerners, the easterners, and the westerners&quot; at the time. Of course, if we look at a modern understanding, it gets a lot more complicated, partially because we have to consider what racial-ethnic-cultural divides would have changed between Irem and our knowledge of history. We know that there are the four major groups, so we know that Egyptians and Libyans and Syrians are all considered separate. But within those groups, there's a lot we don't know: where are the Hebrews? Are the Arabs divided into North and South? How different are the Ki-En-Gir from the proto-Assyrians, from the proto-Medes, from the proto-Hittites? The same kinds of questions can be said for the Libyan and Nubian super-groups, except our modern knowledge is lacking in these areas, other than the basic connection of the Libyans with modern Berbers/Amazigh.<br /> <br /> This is also one of the reasons that I prefer to extend the borders of Irem far further than in the core-books, to allow more diversity. Why should Irem be limited to the heights of Ancient Egypt? Why not extend Irem far along the North African coast, as far as the Atlantic? Why not extend it south to the source of the Nile? Why not allow it to encompass the proto-Hittites? Encompass Arabia, the only issue is when you get to the Ki-En-Gir, who one can't really shift from Sumeria without them stopping being the Ki-En-Gir. Thus one can fully include most of the four Egyptian race constructs, and opens up new borders for the odd character; one can have a proto-Cretian character who had contacts with Irem's Anatolian wing.<br /> <br /> Edit; One really good question would be; how would an Iremite see todays ethnicities? How would they adapt to a new way of thinking about types of people?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","user":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","date":". 04-04-2015, 03:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-04T16:32:19-07:00","id":468936,"title":"","content":"Question: I have an Arisen from a proto-Scythian culture, in what is now Iraq. Would he be Byblos or vague barbarian?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-04T17:28:19-07:00","id":468986,"title":"","content":"Proto scythian in Mesopotamia? How?<br /> <br /> Esit: by which I did not mean to sound bitchy, the answer would change my answer. If legit in Mesopotamia then Byblos if not than probably an anomalous random barbarian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","user":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","date":". 04-04-2015, 09:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-04T19:54:57-07:00","id":469093,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=468986#post468986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Proto scythian in Mesopotamia? How?<br /> <br /> Esit: by which I did not mean to sound bitchy, the answer would change my answer. If legit in Mesopotamia then Byblos if not than probably an anomalous random barbarian.</div> </div> </div> </div>Byblos in that it's the closest named ethnic group.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-04T20:57:55-07:00","id":469145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=469093#post469093\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Byblos in that it's the closest named ethnic group.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The question though is why you're associating a proto-Scythian with Mesopotamia. The Scythians were from the opposite side of the Black Sea from Mesopotamia... actually come to think of it, the &quot;proto-Scythians&quot; of said central-Asian-Steppes region, on the north side of the Black Sea, would actually be the theorized forbearers of the Indo-European language family. Timeline wise, it'd work pretty well if it's the fall of Irem that triggers their expansion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 04-05-2015, 12:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-06T21:44:18-07:00","id":506389,"title":"","content":".........I'm concerned it has something to do with this crap....<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/%22Table_of_Nations%22_from_the_Book_of_Gates%2C_4th_Division%2C_5th_Hour%2C_Tomb_of_Seti_I%2C_based_on_an_illustration_by_Ernst_Weidenbach_for_Richard_Lepsius%E2%80%99_Denkm%C3%A4ler_aus_%C3%84gypten_und_%C3%84thiopien.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy\" target=\"_blank\"> Ancient Egyptian race controversy - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy\" target=\"_blank\"> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Specifically..... &quot;Ampim has a specific concern about the painting of the &quot;Table of Nations&quot; in the Tomb of Ramses III (KV11). The &quot;Table of Nations&quot; is a standard painting which appears in a number of tombs, and they were usually provided for the guidance of the soul of the deceased.<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy#cite_note-egyptologyonline.com-77\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">77</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">]</span></a></sup><sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy#cite_note-sacred-texts.com-86\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">86</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">]</span></a></sup> Among other things, it described the &quot;four races of men,&quot; as follows: (translation by E.A. Wallis Budge:<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy#cite_note-sacred-texts.com-86\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">86</span><span style=\"font-size:10px\">]</span></a></sup> &quot;The first are RETH, the second are AAMU, the third are NEHESU, and the fourth are THEMEHU. The RETH are <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptians\" target=\"_blank\">Egyptians</a>, the AAMU are dwellers in the deserts to the east and north-east of Egypt, the NEHESU are the black races, and the THEMEHU are the fair-skinned <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libyans\" target=\"_blank\">Libyans</a>.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T12:51:32-07:00","id":507095,"title":"","content":"Here's the thing though: you don't have to assign any of the groups above to modern racial groupings, all of which try to claim the Ancient Egyptians as part of their own (mostly in response to asshole colonialists claiming that they had to be white because no black people could have created such monuments, see also claims that white people must have created the Mississippi Mound culture and other similar racist bilge). But outside of the need to give your current political project a link to a 'more glorious past', does it really MATTER the colour of their skin? What matters is how they divided the world, which you can argue was just as much geographical (those to the east, west, south, and centre) as it was based on skin colour. So in that sense, Ancient Egyptians were not Black, nor were they White. They most likely had lighter skin than the Nubians, yet darker than the Greeks and definitely darker than any Celtic or Germanic peoples, yet they were not capital B Black nor capital W White, because such ideas were irrelevant to their conceptions of the world.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T13:17:24-07:00","id":507108,"title":"","content":"There really isn't much of a controversy about the ethnicity of ancient Egyptians, which covered a range of appearances common in North Africa. Ancient and modern Egyptians share a strong genetic relationship, though Egyptians, like most people, have ancestors from other places after thousands of years of history.<br /> <br /> However, there are explosive political aspects obscuring much of this, especially in the popular imagination. Early racists claimed ancient Egyptians were whites. Modern Afrocentrists have made claims in the modern period. Arguments that modern Egyptians share little to no kinship with ancient Egyptians has been used to push various political stances as well.<br /> <br /> One thing to note is that to the ancients, the closest thing to &quot;race&quot; would really mean &quot;some characteristics we associate with a nationality partly out of fact, partly out of cultural tradition.&quot; Some of the distinctions made in the Table of Nations might be invisible to many of us, and the Egyptians may have exaggerated them in art for effect, just as we produce exaggerated skin tones and features in often-problematic art now. It also means that if someone came from beyond the regions currently known but generally looked like someone from such and such a place, they'd be slotted into that category, though maybe seen as an extreme type (by Egyptian/Iremite standards). If they looked a lot different, they would be considered some sort of unclassifiable foreigner. The root of the bias would be nationality, not really ancestry after the fashion of modern racism. So if you're from past Ki-En-Gir they'll still say you have that &quot;type&quot; as long as you vaguely look the part.<br /> <br /> As for what Arisen think of modern ethnicity? Virtually none of them would be &quot;white-passing&quot; in their original bodies, they almost all believe their African nation was the greatest civilization that can ever exist, and they practiced bias based on national political rivalries, so their attitudes are probably at right angles to any opinion anybody's had for centuries. Basically, they know there are actual gods, so everybody who doesn't is sort of an idiot for not following them anyway.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":164,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","user":"Malcolm","date":".  Tweaking some phrasing 05-07-2015, 11:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T19:31:05-07:00","id":507501,"title":"","content":"Some other cultures that might have been very influential in the Iremite world -<br /> <br /> Proto-Dilmun : This verdant paradise of wetlands (now some of the most desolate places on earth, hmm) was so pretty that the people in Ki-En-Gir describe it as paradise and envision the Gods retired here. The legend of the Garden of Eden probably comes from this region. This legendary civilization is on the Persian gulf, right next to Ki-En-Gir.<br /> <br /> Proto-Ubar : Ubar is NOT the Ki-En-Gir. Not sure what happened here?... but I assume the mummies misremember, or something, because Ubar is ACTUALLY on the southern edge of Saudi Arabia and is likewise extensively involved in the trade of exotic gums (particularly frankincense). Ubar is actually quite some distance from Ki-En-Gir, separated geographically by the proto-Dilmun civilization.<br /> <br /> Proto-Indus : The pre-Harappan culture was producing a wide variety of art and fine ceramics at this time. The Indus Valley represented the eastern end of the 'Gum Road', which saw impressive amounts of trade given that people were using essentially overland routes and reed boats. By following monsoonal winds, merchants had a seasonal way of getting too and from western lands like Ubar and Irem.<br /> <br /> Proto-Punt : Also gum traders (the incense thing was huge in the ancient world, much like spices), the civilization of Punt... mythical to the Egyptians themselves, explored by Hatshepsut in her famous expidition... would have factored large in the lives of Iremites, assuming they didn't outright just conquer them, which historical information would suggest they did not bother to do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T21:03:46-07:00","id":507603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=507501#post507501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Proto-Ubar : Ubar is NOT the Ki-En-Gir. Not sure what happened here?... <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>If you definitively know where mythic Ubar was archaeologists would like to speak to you. In any event, the Ki-En-Gir in Mummy is not necessarily historical Sumer.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T21:31:56-07:00","id":507632,"title":"","content":"In point of fact, archeologists do have a pretty good idea where Ubar was, as well as several major cities that made it up (such as Saffara and what would be modern Moscha).<br /> <br /> Even if you don't accept that research about a specific location as valid (Ptolemy's map is pretty iffy in many respects and Ubar is called by another name), Ubar is a distinctively Arabic myth that is local to southern Saudi Arabia. The mythical city of Ubar is an old tribal legend about a ruin that supposadly is in the empty quarter, which is... again... in southern Saudi Arabia, nowhere near Ki-En-Gir.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T22:29:07-07:00","id":507687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=507632#post507632\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In point of fact, archeologists do have a pretty good idea where Ubar was, as well as several major cities that made it up (such as Saffara and what would be modern Moscha).<br /> <br /> Even if you don't accept that research about a specific location as valid (Ptolemy's map is pretty iffy in many respects and Ubar is called by another name), Ubar is a distinctively Arabic myth that is local to southern Saudi Arabia. The mythical city of Ubar is an old tribal legend about a ruin that supposadly is in the empty quarter, which is... again... in southern Saudi Arabia, nowhere near Ki-En-Gir.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ubar has been &quot;discovered&quot; in multiple locations, so I felt pretty free to place it generally in middle or southern Arabia, just as I felt perfectly fine changing the extent of a fictionally supernaturally-dominated Sumer/Ki-En-Gir when describing it during the sketchily understood Ubaid period, which is contemporaneous with late Irem. I mean hell, Ubar is supposed to *be* Irem, but in Mummy Irem is in Egypt. Honestly, pointing to archaeological sites as the source of this or that legend is such a dodgy practice to begin with. For instance, archaeology at times and places contemporaneous with the Bible has been significantly interfered with due to assumptions that it would be a matter of confirming this or that. <br /> <br /> In other words, yes, we who write this stuff, do research this stuff. It doesn't mean we'll fictionalize them the way you would have, though. Just as Irem extended beyond Egypt proper, its closest rival had a somewhat longer grasp than its historical counterpart, and due to other ahistorical factors, a much longer reach.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-07T22:32:21-07:00","id":507689,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=507687#post507687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In other words, yes, we who write this stuff, do research this stuff. It doesn't mean we'll fictionalize them the way you would have, though. Just as Irem extended beyond Egypt proper, its closest rival had a somewhat longer <b>grasp</b> than its historical counterpart, and due to other ahistorical factors, a much longer <b>reach</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Beautiful phrasing there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I laughed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-08T00:11:09-07:00","id":507766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=507687#post507687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ubar has been &quot;discovered&quot; in multiple locations, so I felt pretty free to place it generally in middle or southern Arabia, just as I felt perfectly fine changing the extent of a fictionally supernaturally-dominated Sumer/Ki-En-Gir when describing it during the sketchily understood Ubaid period, which is contemporaneous with late Irem. I mean hell, Ubar is supposed to *be* Irem, but in Mummy Irem is in Egypt. Honestly, pointing to archaeological sites as the source of this or that legend is such a dodgy practice to begin with. For instance, archaeology at times and places contemporaneous with the Bible has been significantly interfered with due to assumptions that it would be a matter of confirming this or that.<br /> <br /> In other words, yes, we who write this stuff, do research this stuff. It doesn't mean we'll fictionalize them the way you would have, though. Just as Irem extended beyond Egypt proper, its closest rival had a somewhat longer grasp than its historical counterpart, and due to other ahistorical factors, a much longer reach.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I'm constantly amazed at the depth of research you DID do, so I don't want to come off as critical so much as trying to put forward ideas for those that might find them useful.<br /> <br /> Currently I'm running a game which is actually placed in the Iremite time period, so I've done a considerable (some might say borderline obsessive) amount of research on the topic, and I find often that many of the things that I initially considered fictional on first reading are in fact quite well thought out, so I've been immensely impressed at how well integrated with archeology the game is. That's partially what got me so invested in the first place!<br /> <br /> Would it be correct to interpret from your reply that proto-Ki-En-Gir may have extended to Ubar and even encompassed it?... perhaps in direct rulership, or maybe just a tributary or trade partner? I've placed the Cursed Citadel in the empty quarter and it's a story-relevant location so I'd be tickled to hear an author interpretation of that concept.<br /> <br /> I've also been thinking quite a bit on how the Rite may have been interpreted as a Great Deluge, since the Deluge pops up in so many places around the exact same historical period. It seems intuitive to me that this massive weather disturbance could have had an origin in Irem...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-08T14:20:17-07:00","id":508418,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I'd say that theories that 'Ubar is solidly in the Arabian peninsula is solid. Or at the very least, the ruins inspiring it are. Then again, 'Ubar is also considered by many to be the same as Iram of the Pillars, one being oral legend the other being Qur'anic, and Iram of the Pillars was one of the largest inspirations for Irem, as should be obvious from the names, but yeah, it's in southern Saudi Arabia or Oman, not near Ki-En-Gir. All the oral evidence points there, as do the handful of excavated or partially excavated ancient cities that could be inspirations for the myths.<br /> <br /> edit: well there was an entire second page that I didn't see until after I hit publish, so I feel kinda stupid now. I thought I was gonna be all smart and go &quot;hey I'm a scholar of the middle east&quot; but instead I was all dumb and &quot;hey I'm not good at 'puters&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-08T15:27:14-07:00","id":508486,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/467762-four-races-of-irem?p=507766#post507766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm constantly amazed at the depth of research you DID do, so I don't want to come off as critical so much as trying to put forward ideas for those that might find them useful.<br /> <br /> Currently I'm running a game which is actually placed in the Iremite time period, so I've done a considerable (some might say borderline obsessive) amount of research on the topic, and I find often that many of the things that I initially considered fictional on first reading are in fact quite well thought out, so I've been immensely impressed at how well integrated with archeology the game is. That's partially what got me so invested in the first place!<br /> <br /> Would it be correct to interpret from your reply that proto-Ki-En-Gir may have extended to Ubar and even encompassed it?... perhaps in direct rulership, or maybe just a tributary or trade partner? I've placed the Cursed Citadel in the empty quarter and it's a story-relevant location so I'd be tickled to hear an author interpretation of that concept.<br /> <br /> I've also been thinking quite a bit on how the Rite may have been interpreted as a Great Deluge, since the Deluge pops up in so many places around the exact same historical period. It seems intuitive to me that this massive weather disturbance could have had an origin in Irem...</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Sorry, I suppose I did come off as snippy. Yeah, Ki-En-Gir in the game has much larger area of influence due to supernatural factors and the Arabian peninsula can be considered a conflict area between it and Irem, but I don't want to close off possibilities by being too specific about things like political relationships. We know for example that the Nameless Empire sent tax farmers/judges in wide migratory loops to reinforce their rule. The Nameless Empire was distinct in possessing centralized power to the extent that it did. For now though, the intent is that Ki-En-Gir is really a loosely defined part of the backstory amenable to your additions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-11T07:14:34-07:00","id":510610,"title":"","content":"If Ubar was Araban territory that was formerly under the sway of the Ki-En-Gir city-states, but later taken by Irem, it would explain why some souces conflate Ubar and Irem and others don't (as well as why some treat Ubar as a city and some as a region).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-11T10:14:14-07:00","id":510862,"title":"","content":"This is why I love mummy. This game seriously encourages the storyteller to become a mini-history buff. I swear, I feel smarter since it came out. As for the proto-scythians, in my stories, theyre the ones who &quot;stopped&quot; Irem from flooding uo over the Caucasus Mountains and Black sea. Even with its organized slave-army, the steppe nomads were too spread out, had no single king to conquer, could move away.. yet also, could take out any Iremite outposts guerilla style. I have it slated that the Iremites learned horsemanship and chariot use from them (once Irem fell, it'd take a long time for the Horse-tech to return to its former territories). Arabia and the persian deserts were similarly &quot;border zones&quot; where Irem could barely get a foothold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}